WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.280
Amongst themselves for a 30 minutes for around&nbsp;
about 30 minutes and then we will follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.800
up with a 30 minutes question and answer session&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.880
and at this point you'll have an opportunity to&nbsp;
put forward your questions yourself to the panel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:30.320
So I'll be calling the names, and then you'll&nbsp;
get an opportunity to to ask your questions.&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.360
I think some of you may have submitted questions&nbsp;
before so the panel will try we've only got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.680
short slot which also I'll keep quiet in a minute&nbsp;
but then the panel will try and answer all your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.920
questions to the best they can. So, we'll try&nbsp;
and fit quite a lot in. So without further ado,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:55.440
I will call on the first speaker Dr. Arday, may I&nbsp;
ask you to lead on your five minutes provocation?&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:01.360
Thank you that's great. Thank you so much everyone&nbsp;
good morning. I hope you're all keeping well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.840
Good morning Councillor Williams and to the&nbsp;
rest of the panel. It's really good to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.560
everyone and thanks for everyone's taking&nbsp;
the time to be here on a Thursday morning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:21.600
I guess we're living in a really turbulent time&nbsp;
right now in terms of how we define Britishness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:29.160
and belonging. The hostile environment&nbsp;
is thriving at the expense of Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.880
and ethnic minority people so it's really&nbsp;
interesting to think about this idea of history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.000
and in particular British history.&nbsp;
And what comprises that history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:46.240
and the kind of historical amnesia that&nbsp;
ensues when we're talking about history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.840
generally speaking, which completely omits the&nbsp;
contribution of Black and ethnic minorities to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.560
Empire, British legacy, British infrastructure,&nbsp;
the economy - all of those things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.360
over many centuries. So, I guess the&nbsp;
thing I want to talk about is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.920
what that looks like in our educational spaces.&nbsp;
And I suppose the thing that becomes really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.760
pertinent when considering these things, is&nbsp;
that sense of belonging within the classroom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:20.800
And education as itself is a microcosm of society.&nbsp;
So it's this microcosm of society yet we have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:28.560
cannon of education that isn't actually reflective&nbsp;
of a multicultural, multi-ethnic, multi-diverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.360
society. So when individuals are going into that&nbsp;
classroom space, there is a disconnect and an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.320
absence in terms of belonging because you're not&nbsp;
actually seeing aspects of your own diaspora,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.280
indigenous populous, lived experience reflected&nbsp;
in the curriculum that you're engaging with. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:49.840
I think one of the things that becomes really&nbsp;
important is the impact upon attainment. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:57.200
Black students, ethnic minority students,&nbsp;
against what is inherently an institutionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:03.600
racist and disadvantaging education system have&nbsp;
flourished. Have flourished over the last 50 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.560
60 years as it's noted in the Robbins Report&nbsp;
(1963), the Scarman Report (1981) and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:15.600
reports that have resurfaced since then probably&nbsp;
most latterly the MacPherson Report in 1999. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.200
one of the recommendations under the first report&nbsp;
in 1999 in terms of becoming racially literate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:30.400
racially cognizant was this understanding around&nbsp;
embedding Black history within our national&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:37.920
curriculum as a British history not as a separate&nbsp;
history, devolved of a white dominant Eurocentric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.320
narrative. So, I think that becomes really,&nbsp;
really important because what we are seeing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.560
an education system that is systematically&nbsp;
failing, particularly young Black boys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.640
And I think it's also important to acknowledge&nbsp;
that it's to everyone's benefit to have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:03.120
type of knowledge, because it facilitates&nbsp;
the society we live in. The whole purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:07.840
of education, in many ways, is to prepare people&nbsp;
to take their place within society. And education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:16.880
is failing all of its inhabitants by not providing&nbsp;
them with the tools, the information, they need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:26.560
circumnavigate or to navigate, a multicultural and&nbsp;
diverse society. From an attainment point of view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:32.880
what we are seeing is that absence of cognition&nbsp;
and relation to looking at a curriculum that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:41.520
actually "I'm seeing people that look like me"&nbsp;
that moves beyond entertainment or music. That has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.800
always been funneled into one area. I think it's&nbsp;
really important we think about the contributions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.320
of Black and ethnic minority people, particularly&nbsp;
over the last 100 years to the British context.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.840
In terms of attainment, what we are seeing are&nbsp;
deficits that really revealed themselves, not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.280
in secondary education but all the way through to&nbsp;
higher education. And what we're seeing is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:11.280
while we continue to have ethnic minority students&nbsp;
going to university in record numbers, just under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:18.720
half the university population in the UK are&nbsp;
Black and ethnic minorities, the outcomes still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.400
continue to be pretty poor in terms of the actual&nbsp;
attainment gap which for the higher education&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:33.440
remains at 23 per cent. There have been&nbsp;
institutions that have managed to reduce that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:39.920
to varying degrees, but fundamentally what we're&nbsp;
still struggling with is that deficit in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:47.200
how all people access education, and how we&nbsp;
omit particular types of education and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:53.360
centrality of a dominant, white, euro-centric&nbsp;
knowledge cannon, which completely omits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.720
and marginalizes different types of knowledge.&nbsp;
And in doing that, and having those kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:04.080
gatekeepers, and those kind of custodians, what&nbsp;
we're actually doing is further placing Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:09.920
and ethnic minority people on the periphery of&nbsp;
educational spaces and having better educational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:16.080
outcomes. So, I think the most important thing&nbsp;
is, for us as a collective and moving forward, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:22.640
think about how we change those spaces to ensure&nbsp;
better outcomes for young Black people and ethnic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:28.800
minorities. I think the pipeline between education&nbsp;
and prison is one that probably needs to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:33.840
forensically more examined. Because the high&nbsp;
exclusion rate of young black boys for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:41.360
between the ages of 13 and 16, do lead them on&nbsp;
that pathway to that. We need to think about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:48.480
the education system is actually facilitating&nbsp;
that because, in terms of the 2011 census,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:55.200
14 per cent of the population are Black and ethnic&nbsp;
minorities 3 per cent are Black, but you still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:07:00.240
have a huge percentage of that 3 per cent that&nbsp;
make up a lot of the prison population in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:05.920
UK. So it's thinking about how we disrupt those&nbsp;
patterns and thinking about aspects of curriculum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:11.920
and attainment. How we establish belonging&nbsp;
pedagogically in the classroom become really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:17.680
essential in terms of doing that. I think that's&nbsp;
me so thank you so much and I'm really looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:22.080
forward to hearing what everyone else has to say.&nbsp;
So thank you for your time, I'm really grateful

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.280
Hi everyone. I think I'm next am I?

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:42.800
Martha you're definitely next. Please go ahead.&nbsp;
Okay you just appeared, sorry about that. Without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.640
an introduction. Hi everybody. This is Martha&nbsp;
Holder, one of our primary Head Teachers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.960
Please go ahead and introduce yourself properly.&nbsp;
Thank you. Okay, thank you. Thank you for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.600
I mean it's a real privilege to be on this&nbsp;
panel and to be asked to be here today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:05.280
I think that in terms of what's happening,&nbsp;
I'd like to just reiterate and just share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:14.160
my experiences of COVID and of Black Lives Matter.&nbsp;
I was deeply, deeply shocked by what happened in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.640
terms of, I wouldn't say a murder, I would say&nbsp;
an execution, that took place in front of us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:26.800
And many millions of us were able to see that. And&nbsp;
I think in terms of that, it's really important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:31.520
that the work that we're doing, does continue.&nbsp;
And I know that that's been reiterated by most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:36.640
of the panel with regards to that. I'd like&nbsp;
to say, that I think that Greenwich is doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:43.600
a great job in acknowledging what took place.
They had the installation back in, I think it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:50.640
June, down by Tesco's, which had the big poster on&nbsp;
the floor for Black Lives Matter. And as a school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:55.920
we really do try to tackle those difficult&nbsp;
conversations with our children. So during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:02.400
COVID-19, we set some homework for the children,&nbsp;
it was by Google because obviously not all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.040
children are in school, where children really got&nbsp;
to understand the difference between Black Lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:13.360
Matter, and All Lives Matter. And it's about us&nbsp;
really being brave and taking a risk, and actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:19.680
having those conversations and giving the children&nbsp;
the platform to share their experiences and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.840
to really make informed decisions, not just&nbsp;
stereotypes or reiterate what they've heard.&nbsp;

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:32.960
I think that in terms of education, I feel&nbsp;
really optimistic about education. I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:40.080
there's so many opportunities for us to really&nbsp;
change how we can change how things happen in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:46.320
past, and how we can shape the future as a school.&nbsp;
We don't do Black History Month per se, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:52.240
we think it's part of the whole curriculum.&nbsp;
So we have diversity throughout the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:59.760
This year, this term, we're doing diversity&nbsp;
looking at Diversity, Power and Politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:06.080
and it's throughout the school from Year 1&nbsp;
reading the story Amazing Grace, to Year 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.200
looking at really challenging texts. So,&nbsp;
I think that there is a lot of work that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:14.960
we can do, and a lot of things that we&nbsp;
can do, to really empower our children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.960
But we do know that the statistics we do know,&nbsp;
fundamentally that something happens in primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.600
school and something happens when the children get&nbsp;
to secondary school. And it's a conversation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:32.800
has been going on for decades. I would say&nbsp;
when I was at university, the Swan Report&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.960
spoke about the disparity between young&nbsp;
Black boys and the education system. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:43.040
I think it's really important that we&nbsp;
have people from diverse backgrounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:49.760
that children see themselves as educators.&nbsp;
And I'd like to add on with what Adel said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:55.840
that it goes beyond a superstar, it goes beyond&nbsp;
somebody singing, it goes beyond somebody being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:02.720
a sports person. But we look at those different&nbsp;
industries that Black people are marginalized in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:09.040
and actually challenge that and allow children&nbsp;
to have experience of people from different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:16.640
backgrounds doing different roles. We were really&nbsp;
fortunate in terms of the children in our school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:27.440
that we were able to work with a lady called Grace&nbsp;
who has put together a great maths game 'Race to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:35.440
Infinity'. Children need to experience, know that&nbsp;
their experiences are acknowledged and the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:41.280
way that really does happen, really fundamentally,&nbsp;
is if they can relate to people, if people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:49.280
really recognize their experiences. So in terms&nbsp;
of secondary school children, and the experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:54.640
of our Black children in secondary school, it's&nbsp;
really important that we speak to them so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:59.600
it's not just a panel making decisions but&nbsp;
their voice is heard. I think we have to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:06.400
aspirations for all of our children, and be really&nbsp;
ambitious and fight for what they they deserve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:12.080
Children at Mulgrave deserve the best education.&nbsp;
I know that Greenwich has high expectations of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:17.440
children. The Director [of Children's Services],&nbsp;
Florence Kroll, has set up a Black Lives Matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:23.280
working party, which says a lot. We will&nbsp;
be tackling really difficult issues, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.320
challenging issues, and it will be uncomfortable.&nbsp;
I think we have to be able to get uncomfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:35.120
to move forward. But I would like to hope that we&nbsp;
are all really ambitious for the children. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:40.240
know that I can speak for Mulgrave School, that&nbsp;
we only expect the best for our children. We want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:44.960
to give them the best. And I can say that about&nbsp;
other schools in Greenwich as well. So, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:51.840
is a lot of work to do. It is a challenge. But I&nbsp;
think that there is the possibility to really make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:56.720
that fundamental change, and give children a love&nbsp;
of education, and for them to realize that it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:02.400
really take them to real far, far gone places.&nbsp;
The world's getting smaller now with COVID-19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:06.640
we can't travel as much, but I still want&nbsp;
children to be ambitious about travelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:11.840
and going to other places and being in careers&nbsp;
that they may not have considered before.&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:17.920
Thank you very much Ms. Holder. Sorry about&nbsp;
that. I didn't introduce you properly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:25.440
Thank you. Thank you Dr. Arday for sharing&nbsp;
your five minutes' thoughts at this time.&nbsp;

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:39.680
I'm going to invite Dr. Jivraj to speak to us.&nbsp;
Is Dr. Jivraj here? I can't see her online.&nbsp;

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:47.600
Hi, yes. I was just waiting to be unmuted.&nbsp;
Okay I think this works now. Can everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:55.200
hear me? Someone give me a thumbs up. Yeah, okay.&nbsp;
Great, thank you so much for inviting me to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.200 --> 00:14:01.120
to Greenwich today. I'm from the University of&nbsp;
Kent. I'm based in the Law School there and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:09.040
have a campus in Chatham Maritime along with the&nbsp;
University of Kent, so there are connections there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:14.880
which are particularly personal to me because&nbsp;
I've spent a long time teaching on that campus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:20.160
and getting to know a lot of students who&nbsp;
come there from the Greenwich vicinity.

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:48.640
Great, so as I said I'm in the Law School&nbsp;
and I teach public law which is one of the,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.360 --> 00:14:54.640
it's the flagship module that all our&nbsp;
incoming students do in their first year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:04.080
It's a compulsory module. We have a cohort&nbsp;
between 450 to 700 depending on the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:13.680
So, it's a real privilege, I think, to be able to&nbsp;
have access to these amazing young minds who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:21.360
eager to learn and have their thinking really&nbsp;
challenged in this new university context.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:36.160
And as part of that module, I introduce them into&nbsp;
various comparative and international ideas of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:45.920
constitutional law. How democracy works. How the&nbsp;
history of democracy, but not just in relation to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:50.960
what we're used to hearing about, so for example&nbsp;
the French and the American revolutions, and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:57.840
the Enlightenment period gave rise to democracy as&nbsp;
we understand it now in the modern era, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:05.840
in relation to post-apartheid South Africa,&nbsp;
in relation to indigenous moves towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:16.000
constitutionality in South America, the slave&nbsp;
rebellions in Haiti which gave rise to the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:22.960
constitution, modern constitution, as we know it.&nbsp;
And, so they get this kind of barrage from me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:27.280
of all this information that they're&nbsp;
not expecting, because it's so different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:35.840
from what they're used to in terms of a very&nbsp;
white curriculum. And, so by the time they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:42.400
reach my Third Year module, which is an optional&nbsp;
module, I get students who are really keen to put,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:49.840
get their teeth into, this material in much more&nbsp;
detail. And that module is Race, Religion and Law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:57.200
And more recently, we've been taking a very&nbsp;
intersectional approach to it, so thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:05.760
the intersections between race, religion, gender&nbsp;
and sexuality, as well. And, what I hear by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:12.960
time the third year students reach me, is "Wow,&nbsp;
why did we not do more of this in first year?",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:20.320
"Why don't I know about this history about how&nbsp;
the law was constituted and how it's perpetuated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:26.640
so much violence against black people and people&nbsp;
of colour across the world?". And not only that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:29.840
so it's not just you know the&nbsp;
Zong case which talks about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.320 --> 00:17:38.640
how slave traders were compensated for&nbsp;
supposed loss of cargo, basically murdering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.640 --> 00:17:46.880
of slaves on board these slave ships,&nbsp;
but also in relation to knowledge that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.440 --> 00:18:01.360
is out there coming from amazing lawyers&nbsp;
in Africa, Asia. And that they don't know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:06.320
about this knowledge that is produced and I heard&nbsp;
Claire Alexander recently who I know works with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:18.560
Jason [Dr. Arday], and she was saying that we have&nbsp;
this perception that information isn't out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:25.120
we need to go digging for Black history, or Black&nbsp;
scholars of this body of scholarship. That we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:32.400
so fixated, and the fact that the white canon&nbsp;
is all there is, and that's simply not true.&nbsp;

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:40.640
And so bringing this other knowledge to students&nbsp;
at all levels, not just in higher education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:48.160
but at all levels, which wonderful organizations&nbsp;
like The Black Curriculum, Kids of Color and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.160 --> 00:18:54.480
initiatives are now doing and calling upon the&nbsp;
government I think is really, really essential.&nbsp;

00:18:55.280 --> 00:19:00.800
So as a lawyer, I'm just going to remind you,&nbsp;
because I actually have to do this with my own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:04.720
colleagues in in higher education&nbsp;
in Law Schools, and remind them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:14.080
that we are under a legal duty under the Equality&nbsp;
Act, under the Public Sector Equality Duty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:23.600
to provide education in a non-discriminatory&nbsp;
way. Certainly in Higher Education, we've had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:29.600
the recent Equality and Human Rights Commission&nbsp;
inquiry into racial harassment, and this is really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:36.000
the latest report on a plethora, a barrage,&nbsp;
of reports. So, we know there's a problem.&nbsp;

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:44.880
We also know about attainment gaps both in&nbsp;
primary and secondary education, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.800 --> 00:19:53.920
in higher education. And the terminology that's&nbsp;
used is, attainment gaps. Actually, the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.920 --> 00:20:01.520
I heard that term used in a departmental meeting,&nbsp;
I was absolutely outraged and I was thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:08.720
"What what are you talking about?". I prefer&nbsp;
to use the language of attack, not 'attainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:16.240
disparities' but 'awarding disparities', because&nbsp;
the problem as many have have written about, Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:22.960
[Dr. Arday], Robbie Shilliam and many others, is&nbsp;
that it's contingent on this idea of students as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:31.760
somehow lacking. Somehow having a cultural, and&nbsp;
educational, and other forms of deficit. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:40.640
actually in that discourse, there is no mention&nbsp;
of institutional racism, of systemic racism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:48.640
and really that's what is part of what I'm doing,&nbsp;
is trying to shift some of the focus on to that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:59.280
and I'll come on to that in a minute.
I was awarded some funding by the Socio-Legal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:09.120
Studies Association, to put together the resource&nbsp;
that I recently launched. It's available. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:15.840
an anti-racist, legal pedagogy resource.&nbsp;
It's relevant though beyond just the law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:24.000
and it's available for free to download.&nbsp;
I think the link is on the next slide.&nbsp;

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:32.000
As part of that, l interviewed law teachers&nbsp;
who I knew were already doing amazing critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:37.600
scholarship and teaching. And one&nbsp;
of the interviewees said to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.600 --> 00:21:42.560
"the attainment gap as it's known is&nbsp;
definitely a product of what we teach".&nbsp;

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:46.960
What we can do in the university,&nbsp;
and I'd say education more broadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:53.760
is to reflect on what we're teaching, how&nbsp;
we're teaching, and also, how we're assessing.&nbsp;

00:21:53.760 --> 00:22:00.480
And when I saw this photo from&nbsp;
one of the BLM protests it really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:07.840
it talks to that point, that Martha mentioned&nbsp;
about discomfort, which I'm going to come back to.&nbsp;

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:14.000
Actually, yes, people find it scary, but we&nbsp;
need to remind ourselves that as educators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:21.200
it's a privilege to educate yourself about racism,&nbsp;
instead of actually having to experience it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:24.160
so let's contemplate and&nbsp;
reflect on that for a moment.

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:34.480
Here are some of my wonderful students. So&nbsp;
the project 'Decolonizing the Curriculum'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:41.600
is unique in the sense that most decolonizing the&nbsp;
curriculum projects are either student movements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.560 --> 00:22:47.440
or institutional initiatives&nbsp;
that come under the guise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:52.720
equality, diversity and inclusion, or student&nbsp;
success, as they're more recently calling it.&nbsp;

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:58.400
This project, which I&nbsp;
initiated, was with my students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:07.600
This project I initiated with the students that I&nbsp;
teach, because as this discourse around attainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:13.520
gaps was coming out, and becoming bigger, I was&nbsp;
getting really angry. I was discussing it with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:19.520
students in class, because it didn't reflect&nbsp;
my experience of the students that I teach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:28.320
So, I put together a funding bid for&nbsp;
some Innovation in Teaching funds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:36.720
and we were awarded it. Essentially, the students&nbsp;
conducted their own research. So what was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:43.280
interesting and innovative about this project,&nbsp;
is that I and my colleagues trained the students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:49.840
to become researchers. So they went through&nbsp;
a whole process, from ethics approval, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:57.440
literature reviews, to writing skills, research&nbsp;
skills, focus group training skills, and then they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.080 --> 00:24:04.400
led these focus groups with the other students&nbsp;
and and they led them in what we called "Colonial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:10.160
Cafe" style environments, and they led. They&nbsp;
chose the theme, so they had specific groups for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:18.960
Black men, Muslim women, around mental health,&nbsp;
around well-being, international students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:24.240
so they really focused in on some of the issues&nbsp;
that they felt were most pressing for them.&nbsp;

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:34.560
They created this manifesto and they&nbsp;
launched it at a conference in March 2019.&nbsp;

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:41.600
The link on the bottom of the slide there you can&nbsp;
see the manifesto footage from the conference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:45.840
and the other resources that they've&nbsp;
created - a reading list and so on.&nbsp;

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:52.000
They went on to actually train&nbsp;
staff, so their expertise and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.720 --> 00:25:01.760
knowledge went into their training staff, both&nbsp;
within our university and also externally.&nbsp;

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:04.960
And, actually one of the students,&nbsp;
Lisa, you can see they're presenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:10.880
the manifesto to the Speaker of the&nbsp;
House of Commons, she has recorded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:18.080
a lovely video about the project, which you can&nbsp;
see on that page and recounts it in more detail.&nbsp;

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:26.480
I would encourage you to go and see for yourself.&nbsp;
Someone who started off as a undergraduate student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:33.120
went through quite a difficult time, has been&nbsp;
part of this process, and is now completing her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:37.040
Master's in Research so you know as Jason&nbsp;
[Dr. Arday] said, there's that pipeline.&nbsp;

00:25:38.480 --> 00:25:44.000
The other wonderful thing about the project was&nbsp;
that it really continued, or helped to continue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.000 --> 00:25:49.440
the pipeline and the interaction between&nbsp;
the students themselves, creating a sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:56.720
belonging and taking up space. There's more I can&nbsp;
say about how we did how we went about creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.520 --> 00:26:04.800
what we call 'principal spaces' rather than 'safe&nbsp;
spaces', because we can't guarantee safe spaces so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.800 --> 00:26:08.320
you know working through principles,&nbsp;
and all the information is on the web.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:19.920
Coming back to this idea of discomfort, as&nbsp;
we were engaging in this project, there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:20.560 --> 00:26:23.840
everything that we were doing with the&nbsp;
students to get the word out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:28.400
but also a lot of Law School teachers were&nbsp;
coming to me and saying, "Listen, you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:34.320
what you're doing! This looks great. How&nbsp;
can I do something? How can I get started?"&nbsp;

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:41.600
There's a real kind of sense of be feeling&nbsp;
stuck, and not knowing how to get started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:49.040
and that's why I put together this resource&nbsp;
which has a set of prompts, readings, cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:55.760
for law school teachers, for teachers to think&nbsp;
about in getting started in their foundational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.480 --> 00:27:01.520
subjects, where they often think "oh, we don't&nbsp;
have enough time. I'm already overloaded".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:13.840
It's meant to be a getting started process&nbsp;
for those who really have the motivation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:22.960
but don't know how, or where to get started.
I think a key thing that comes out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:31.840
policy that we really need to remember, and in&nbsp;
particular, this report that came out in 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:36.960
and in the bottom corner there in the&nbsp;
black hoodie you'll see one of my students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:44.960
In the report they make five recommendations. And&nbsp;
one of the recommendations is that universities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.960 --> 00:27:50.560
need to have conversations about race.
And this includes whiteness, because race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:57.680
is not just about people of colour, and someone&nbsp;
else's issue, actually, it's white people's issue.&nbsp;

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:05.760
Whiteness and systemic racism, and leadership,&nbsp;
within these institutions. It is an issue of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:15.840
whiteness, and we really need to look at that.
So, a big chunk of the resource is also about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:24.080
how to get started in looking at the issues where&nbsp;
you are. What are the blockages? People obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.200 --> 00:28:32.240
think they know what racism is and recently&nbsp;
there's the term racial microaggression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.280 --> 00:28:38.960
and you've got the work of people like Dr.&nbsp;
Nicola Rollock, and the brilliant work by Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:49.600
Remi Joseph-Salisbury, on this issue of racism,&nbsp;
and we really need to acknowledge how we're all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.400 --> 00:28:59.840
complicit in these behaviors in various ways.
So this defensiveness and discomfort about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:04.560
actually being willing to acknowledge how we&nbsp;
are complicit, that really is the first hurdle.&nbsp;

00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:10.080
Before we can get to thinking about how we can&nbsp;
change our curriculum and all the rest of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:21.200
I really think that we need to reflect on&nbsp;
where where we are at, and what we're engaging.&nbsp;

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:28.800
Students are telling us this all the time, "I'm&nbsp;
not just uncomfortable with what I'm being taught,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:33.200
I'm uncomfortable in the classroom. How&nbsp;
I'm being treated, how I'm being profiled".&nbsp;

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:49.600
This is a wonderful kind of diagram that the&nbsp;
students, and artists, and other experts, came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:55.520
together to put together, as a kind of process -&nbsp;
a stepping stone process - that is continual. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:55.520 --> 00:30:02.240
it's not a journey you begin and then you end. No,&nbsp;
it's continual. We're all always on this process.&nbsp;

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:11.840
There's much more on stepping stones, that you&nbsp;
can see on the web page so please do have a look.&nbsp;

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:17.840
I'm sure I've gone over time so I just want to&nbsp;
finish with this event, which is happening this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:24.800
afternoon. It's to celebrate the launch of&nbsp;
the book that came out of the 'Decolonizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:30.640
the Curriculum' project. So, we worked with&nbsp;
the students who were part of the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:30.640 --> 00:30:38.720
to co-author this book and they are so excited&nbsp;
to be co-authors to have a publication.&nbsp;

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:45.520
If you have time to tune in to the&nbsp;
launch, you'll see them talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:51.600
their experiences. What they've achieved,&nbsp;
but also what still needs to be done.&nbsp;

00:30:52.960 --> 00:31:00.160
So, all I can say to end is that it&nbsp;
is an absolute privilege to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:08.880
our students, and we owe it to them to give them&nbsp;
the opportunity to realize this this amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:17.840
potential that they have. And as they say to me,&nbsp;
"Suhraiya, I need to self-actualize." Thank you.

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:28.000
Thank you very much, Dr. Jivraj, a lot to take in.&nbsp;
There are a lot of points that we could follow up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:37.120
on, so really interesting piece of work.
I just want to recognize that you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:41.040
acknowledged in your work, that&nbsp;
the deficit between the attainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:47.920
has a lot to do with the award system, and that&nbsp;
is something that we all need to pay attention to.&nbsp;

00:31:50.080 --> 00:32:02.240
Without any further ado, I just&nbsp;
want to finally bring to you, Ms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:02.960 --> 00:32:09.440
Ukoumunne. If you could introduce yourself.&nbsp;
You've got five minutes. Sorry if it's cut short,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.440 --> 00:32:17.120
the previous speaker took a minute or two of your&nbsp;
time. I do apologize if I'll need to stop you.&nbsp;

00:32:18.000 --> 00:32:24.160
No, that's absolutely fine. It's fine because&nbsp;
everybody has actually covered so much of what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:24.160 --> 00:32:29.600
I wanted to say which is perfect. I also want&nbsp;
to thank you for pronouncing my name properly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:31.280 --> 00:32:36.000
There was a pause of a few seconds, and I thought&nbsp;
let's see what's going to happen. It was amazing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:41.360
well done. Thank you, and yes, everyone's covered&nbsp;
a lot of the points that I wanted to raise.&nbsp;

00:32:42.400 --> 00:32:49.440
I think I will just start by saying my&nbsp;
background actually is in African literature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:55.840
and drama. I'm Nigerian - it was really funny&nbsp;
this morning when Adel said congratulations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.840 --> 00:33:01.040
to all the Nigerians out there on Nigerian&nbsp;
Independence Day, I had completely forgotten.&nbsp;

00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:08.320
I think that this is a reminder of the ways in&nbsp;
which we, our cultural memory, our historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:15.200
memory, about things that are important to us,&nbsp;
can sometimes slip by and can be hidden. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:21.200
reason I start there, is that a lot of my work,&nbsp;
because I started teaching in African literature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:28.000
and I currently teach Critical Race Theory,&nbsp;
a lot of my work focuses on the philosophical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:35.920
and epistemological questions related to&nbsp;
literature, and history and black philosophy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.960 --> 00:33:40.080
The reason it's important for me,&nbsp;
is that as somebody said earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:49.600
we as non-white people, are in an arc of history,&nbsp;
particularly I'm going to focus on African people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.600 --> 00:33:54.400
and people from the African diaspora, it's not&nbsp;
because I don't think other people's histories are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.400 --> 00:34:00.720
important or significant, I think they are deeply,&nbsp;
but I think one of the dangers that we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:00.720 --> 00:34:07.440
when we are ensconced within Eurocentric systems,&nbsp;
is that our histories can become muddled and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.440 --> 00:34:14.880
thrown together. The tag BAME for me is not always&nbsp;
useful, because it can sometimes hide a plethora&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:21.520
of inequalities and injustices, particularly when&nbsp;
we think about Black women's experiences. So, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.520 --> 00:34:28.320
do talk a lot about Black boys' experiences, but&nbsp;
we don't talk about how do Black girls experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:37.840
the education system. How do they overcome&nbsp;
problems of gender and racial disadvantage?&nbsp;

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:43.440
We don't talk about how they experience&nbsp;
violence, both epistemological and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:49.360
physical violence. Because they are Black and&nbsp;women, it's really important for me as somebody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:55.680
who's interested in gender, to say maybe when we&nbsp;started talking about decolonizing the curriculum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.680 --> 00:35:00.320
we must also start foregrounding&nbsp;
the experiences of Black women.&nbsp;

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:09.520
As I said, my kind of philosophical approach to&nbsp;
this is to say we are part of, we participate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.520 --> 00:35:17.600
an arc of history, our danger is that we tend to&nbsp;
treat the African or African Caribbean experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:17.600 --> 00:35:18.100
as if it began in 1948. Every time I read an&nbsp;article it says the Windrush docked in 1948 and

00:35:18.100 --> 00:35:32.800
I think, "Look, Black history is older than 1948!".
So, I think that we need to start thinking out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:37.920
ourselves as having an ancient historical&nbsp;
experience. We need to start thinking of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.480 --> 00:35:44.320
this part of our experience, unpleasant as it is&nbsp;
at times, but it also has its joys. Let's face it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:52.000
it's not all misery. We need to start thinking&nbsp;
about that as part of a long process. It's part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.000 --> 00:36:00.320
a trajectory, and you know, my favourite author at&nbsp;
the moment, Ayi Kwei Armah, talks about the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:07.760
we need to look back, we need to look back to&nbsp;
consider the ways that what we have experienced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:14.160
brings value to us. We need to start thinking&nbsp;
about how the value that is brought to us through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:20.960
our experience can be taken into the future, so&nbsp;
we need to think not in the teleological ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:27.760
that Eurocentric history functions. We shouldn't&nbsp;
be thinking about past, present, future. We should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:32.720
be thinking about the ways in which they&nbsp;
are amalgamated. The ways in which the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.280 --> 00:36:40.320
informs the present, and that in turn informs the&nbsp;
future. For me, that's quite critical, because I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:48.320
like other speakers, want to start thinking about&nbsp;
the ways in which we are all creating history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.320 --> 00:36:55.840
all of the time. History is not the preserve of&nbsp;
very rich or powerful people. Ordinary people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.840 --> 00:37:01.200
Black women who are fighting every day to prevent&nbsp;
their Black boys from being excluded from schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:05.760
are actually creating history. They may&nbsp;
not see themselves as historical beings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:11.120
because they don't write their histories. So,&nbsp;
when I teach critical race theory to my students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:11.120 --> 00:37:17.680
I emphasize the importance of&nbsp;
writing diaries, keeping notes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:24.160
writing your histories. In a hundred&nbsp;
years' time, in 200 years' time, I would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:30.960
very saddened if the history that we focus&nbsp;
on, is the history of social services records,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:39.200
or criminal justice records. We have a history,&nbsp;
we participate in and are producing this society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:39.200 --> 00:37:45.040
but it is our responsibility to make&nbsp;
sure that we keep records and narrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:46.720 --> 00:37:53.520
our histories and we leave that for the benefit of&nbsp;
future generations. So, as this was a provocation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.320 --> 00:37:57.520
a provocation piece, I don't know what&nbsp;
that means, but I think I'll give it a go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:08.080
I thought where I'm at. Where my mind is focusing&nbsp;
at the moment, as an intellectual, as a theorist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:17.840
is, I am wondering whether it is possible, it is&nbsp;
possible for us to decolonize the institution?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:24.960
James Baldwin says something very important.&nbsp;
He says, when you are attempting to reform the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:30.240
institutions, whatever institutions they are,&nbsp;
are you attempting to join a burning house?&nbsp;

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:36.320
Now, I don't want to join a burning house. I&nbsp;
am wondering whether we need to start thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:44.800
is it time in the 21st century, for us to start&nbsp;
thinking about building our own institutions. One&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:44.800 --> 00:38:50.720
of the problems that you have when you participate&nbsp;
in the reform of other people's institutions is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:58.800
firstly, you don't have control over the&nbsp;
time-scale of change. This may take 10, 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:04.000
might take a hundred, 200 years. Who can say?&nbsp;
So, you don't have control over the time-scale.&nbsp;

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:09.040
The second thing that I would say is, that&nbsp;
you don't have control over the agenda.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:15.600
And I think that our agenda might be not&nbsp;
that we just want to focus on Black boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:20.960
entertainment. That's important, but we might&nbsp;
want an agenda that focuses on our humanity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:21.520 --> 00:39:26.960
Can we create a world in which violence&nbsp;
no longer exists? Can we create a world in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:26.960 --> 00:39:33.040
which people are not oppressed because of their&nbsp;
economic circumstances? Can we create a world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:39.440
in which the actual physical world that we&nbsp;possess, that we are constantly abusing through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:47.040
the exploitation of resources, can we create&nbsp;
a world for future generations in which these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:54.800
problems no longer exist? So, I think, when I&nbsp;
say can we move away from the idea of reform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.600 --> 00:40:01.360
my favorite author, Harold Cruse,&nbsp;
asks, "Do we need to focus on rebellion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:02.080 --> 00:40:07.200
or should we focus on revolution?"
And the question is one of reform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:07.200 --> 00:40:14.160
And when I say revolution, I say, rethinking the&nbsp;
way that we think about the world. Changing our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:21.600
consciousness. Shifting our consciousness to ask&nbsp;
for more. To request more, and to bring all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.600 --> 00:40:28.960
our experiences to the human table, as it were,&nbsp;
to change the way in which history progresses.&nbsp;

00:40:28.960 --> 00:40:37.840
That's all I have to say guys.&nbsp;
Thank you so much for your time.

